<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Belz</h1><p class="st-meta">1170 CE – 1462 CE (approx.) · capital Belz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Ruthenian principality on the Polish border, passed between Volhynia, Lithuania and Masovia before joining the Polish Crown.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz">Belz</a>, on a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-bug">Bug</a>, was one of the Cherven towns contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Rus'. It became the seat of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of the Volhynian princes in the later 12th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-mstislavich-of-belz">Vsevolod Mstislavich</a>, a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-the-great">Roman Mstislavich</a>, ruled there.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>It was a border land of fortified towns and farmland, part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-volhynia">Kingdom of Galicia-Volhynia</a> in the 13th century. After that state collapsed in the 1340s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz">Belz</a> was contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, then from 1388 held as a fief by the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siemowit-iv-of-masovia">Siemowit IV</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the Masovian line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz">Belz</a> died out in 1462, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">King Casimir IV</a> incorporated it into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1025-1385">Kingdom of Poland</a> (1385-1569) as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz-voivodeship">Belz Voivodeship</a>. The town later became famous as the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz-hasidic-dynasty">Belz Hasidic dynasty</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-belz.jpg" alt="Principality of Belz" width="320" height="328"><figcaption>Image: Augustus-ua · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belz_kniaz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1170 CE – 1462 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Belz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vsevolod Mstislavich, Siemowit IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Belz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1316&amp;focus=principality-of-belz" aria-label="Principality of Belz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5229 427 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Belz lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5549 683L5553 670M5549 683L5571 720M5549 683L5457 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5553 670h0M5571 720h0M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5549 683h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5549 683h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Belz. <a href="/atlas?year=1316&amp;focus=principality-of-belz">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
